1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 Be7 4. Bc4 Nf6 5. e5 Ng4 6. O-O (6. d4 {was
preferred a year later since the reply} d5 {is suspect owing to} 7. exd6 Bxd6
8. Qe2+ Qe7 9. Qxe7+ Kxe7 10. Nc3 {with advantage (Horne-Barden, Hastings 
[Premier Reserves Major 1948/49])}) 6... Nc6 7. d4 d5 8. exd6 Bxd6 9. Re1+ Ne7
{Here the position is given as equal in 'Modern Chess Openings'.} 10. h3 Nf6
11. Ne5 Bxe5 12. dxe5 Qxd1 13. Rxd1 Nh5 14. Nc3 Bf5 15. Be2 Ng3 16. Bb5+ {
The only move to keep equality.} c6 17. Bxf4 Ne4 (17... Nh5 18. Bg5 cxb5 19.
Nxb5 {is in White's favour.}) 18. Nxe4 Bxe4 19. Bd3 Bxd3 20. Rxd3 Ng6 21. Bg3
Rd8 {A complex situation has resolved into an endgame in which White's
isolated pawn is a weakness.} 22. Rad1 Ke7 23. Kf2 Rxd3 24. Rxd3 Rd8 25. Rxd8
Kxd8 26. Ke3 Ke7 27. Ke4 Ke6 28. c4 {[#]} h5 ({Immediately after the game Euwe
pointed out that} 28... h6 {would have threatened 29...Nxe5. Thus 30 Bxe5 f5+
31 Kd4 c5+ (or 31 Kf4 g5+). This pretty combination was new to me, but is one
to remember.}) 29. b3 b6 30. a4 c5 {White has only one way to avoid Zugzwang.}
31. a5 bxa5 {Black too has little option.} 32. Bf2 Nxe5 33. Bxc5 f5+ 34. Ke3 a4
(34... Nc6 35. Kf4 {favours White!}) 35. bxa4 Nxc4+ 36. Kd4 Ne5 37. Bf8 g6 38.
Bg7 Nd7 39. Kc4 Kd6 40. Kb5 Kc7 41. a5 g5 42. Bd4 f4 {Source: "Chess for the
Love of It" by A. R. B. Thomas (Routledge & Kegan Paul, 1973), p30} 1/2-1/2

